Fillets: two roundings ending at the two vertices of a short sharp edge
whose ends would cross are mitred (mitcad#236).

Where two fillets end alone at the two vertices of a sharp edge E (three
sharp edges at each vertex), each with one contact on E's face S and the
other on an edge of E's other face Fv (the face at both ends), and their
contacts on E overlap (the edge is shorter than the two roundings' setbacks
on it together), each end was cut by Fv as if the other were not there:
the second end's cut crossed the first one's on Fv, and the corner failed
("OneCorner : fillets have too big radiuses"). The two blends meet: each
is trimmed by the other along their intersection, from the point where
their contacts on S cross up to Fv, and by Fv from there to its contact on
Fv's edge. E vanishes.

- ChFi3d_Builder::PerformMitredEnd, called by PerformFilletOnVertex before
  the one-stripe corner: recognises the two ends (constant or variable
  radius fillets, both ends AllSame or OnDiff, their surface data ending on
  faces, a contact on E each on the same face S, the other contacts on arcs
  of Fv, the contact of the end at this vertex lying beyond the other's on
  E), and makes both ends at the later of the two vertices (the earlier one
  is left to it). Each end is two curves, stored as the big stripe's end
  of PerformTwoCornerbyInter is: the mitre, the blends' intersection from
  the crossing of the contacts on S (found on their curves on S and refined
  with ChFi3d_ComputesIntPC) to the point where the blends' cuts by Fv cross
  (found on their curves on Fv), shared by both blends; and each blend's
  cut by Fv from there to its contact on Fv's edge. The contacts on S end
  at their crossing; the stripes' ends are marked as in the data
  structure, so ChFi3d_FilDS adds no end curve.
- Two cylinders of one radius whose axes cross (straight roundings of one
  radius at both ends) meet in two ellipses (IntAna_QuadQuadGeo): the
  mitre is the shorter arc of the one through both points, exact. The walk
  of ChFi3d_ComputeCurves, which starts where the two blends touch (both
  tangent to S at the crossing of their contacts), stays for other
  surfaces.

